Stock value.

A product has a name (text), a cost (money), a count (a whole number) and a category (text).

The value of some products (a list of products) is money:
the sum of its cost times its count for each product in the products.

The value of some products (a list of products) in a category (text) is money:
the sum of its cost times its count for each product in the products whose category is the category.

The empty shelves in some products (a list of products) is a list of texts:
its name for each product in the products whose count is 0.

Suppose pens is a product with name "pens", cost 1.5, count 100 and category "office".
Suppose desks is a product with name "desks", cost 120, count 2 and category "furniture".
Suppose chairs is a product with name "chairs", cost 45, count 0 and category "furniture".
Suppose stock is a list of pens, desks and chairs.
For example, the value of stock is 390.
For example, the value of stock in "furniture" is 240.
For example, the empty shelves in stock joined with "," is "chairs".
